focus now on national parks with Sally  Jaramillo taking us to the Lake Mead  recreational area to show us why it is  the six most visited national park in  the entire country in the 1930s the  Hoover Dam was constructed and at that  time that actually transformed the  mighty Colorado River into Lake Mead  which is America's largest reservoir it  was 1935 when people started utilizing  the area for recreation so the National  Park Service helps the Bureau of  Reclamation manage the area of her  recreation and the area was known as the  Boulder Canyon National Recreation Area  Lake Mead spokeswoman Christy Vanover  says Lake Mead National Recreation Area  became the first National Recreation  Area in 1964 so the park is one and a  half million acres where the size of the  state of Delaware within those areas  you're also going to find campgrounds  launch ramps hiking trails and then we  have hundreds of miles of paved and  unpaved roads so people can do lots of  scenic driving as well we have multiple  developed campgrounds and you can do  anything from camping with by pitching a  tent or bring in your RV and do full  hookups with showers and all sorts of  things for you the best part for only  twenty five dollars you and your family  can enjoy the area for a week there's  also services in the park like boat  rentals so if you don't have your own  boat you can rent a boat you can rent a  kayak up stand-up paddleboard even a  house there for out-of-town visitors  Lake Mead is not something expected in  the middle of the desert  I think visitors are always happy when  they come here you can't help but just  be you know fielding that joy when the  Sun is beating on your face and you get  the reflection of the water it is just  absolutely beautiful I don't know it  feels like we're closer to God sunny had  a meal  reporting from Lake Mead an annual pass  that covers both Lake Mead in Lake  Mohave area is $45 for the year
 

